(a) All applications for a license required by this article shall be noted as to the date and exact time of filing; and, after filing, such applications shall be presented and considered by the board in the order of their filing at the next regular monthly meeting of the board, provided 30 days have elapsed after the date of filing and all necessary investigation and advertisement has been completed.
(b) In determining whether or not any license applied for under this division shall be granted, in addition to all the other provisions of this article, in the public interest and welfare, the following will be considered:
(1) The applicant's reputation, character, mental and physical capacity to conduct this business;
(2) If the applicant is a previous holder of a license to sell any alcoholic beverage, whether or not he has violated any law, regulation or ordinance relating to such business;
(3) If the applicant is a previous holder of a license to sell any alcoholic beverage, the manner in which he conducted the business as to the necessity for unusual police observation and inspection in order to prevent the violation of any law, regulation or ordinance relating to such business;
(4) Whether the person has previously had an alcoholic beverage license suspended or revoked;
(5) The nature of and effect on the neighborhood adjacent to the business;
(6) The proximity of the business to churches, schools and hospitals, but in no case shall a license be granted to a business located within 350 yards of a church, school or hospital. All distances shall be measured at the closest point between the entrance to such place of business and the nearest property line of such school, church or hospital; and
(7) Evidence presented to the board of commissioners in opposition to the issuance of a license.
(c) No license shall be granted for any existing building or establishment having a history or reputation for prostitution or other laws or offensive practices, violent activities, gambling, illegal dealing in alcoholic beverages or drugs, or other violations of the law. By history or reputation it is meant that within the 12 months preceding the filing of the application and provided the business is now owned, operated, managed or in any way connected to the person operating the establishment during the time such offenses occurred.
(d) No license shall be granted for the sale of malt beverages or wine at retail where the proposed location does not have a front entrance to the premises that is clearly visible from a public street, road or highway, except as to a private club.
(e) No license shall be granted to a retailer under any provision or section of this article unless the front entrance to the building is clearly visible from a street, road or highway. The front of the building shall have a window; and no screen, blind, curtain, partition, article or thing that shall prevent a clear view into the interior of the building shall be permitted in the window or upon the doors of any building where retail sales are permitted. Each building shall be so lighted such that the interior of the premises is visible day or night, and all parking areas adjacent to or used in connection with the business shall be fully lighted and unobstructed.
(f) No license shall be granted for the sale of malt beverages or wine from a building occupied as a dwelling.
(g) Retail establishments engaged solely in the sale of malt beverages and wine shall be prohibited in the unincorporated areas of the county. A retail establishment may obtain a license for the sale of malt beverages and wine for consumption off premises only (a class B license), provided the applicant or licensee has and maintains a grocery-related business in combination with the sale of malt beverages and wine with a grocery-related inventory, exclusive of malt beverages and wine, of the wholesale value of not less than $2,500.00. Any applicant or licensee may, at the discretion of the board, or upon reasonable demand by any law enforcement officer, be required to produce and exhibit such wholesale receipts supporting the value of the inventory.
(Ord. of 5-28-1985, § VIII; Ord. of 6-27-1995, § I)
